── password_security_test.sql
   ✔ 0a two member logins provisioned, both on a staff-issued temporary password
   ✔ 1a on a temporary password the account reads no transactions, no members and holds no permission
   ✔ 2a calling the retired log_password_changed() is refused
   ✔ 2b and the lock still holds: no data, no permission, flag untouched
   ✔ 2c no audit row falsely claims the password was changed
   ✔ 2d a signed-in caller holds no grant on either half of a password change, nor on the retired RPCs
   ✔ 2e the proof store is in a private schema and unreachable by any API role
   ✔ 3a both halves refuse a non-service-role caller even when the grant is bypassed
   ✔ 4a a completion whose Auth password never changed is refused
   ✔ 4b and the account is left locked, not opened
   ✔ 4c a completion presenting the wrong nonce is refused
   ✔ 4d an expired proof is refused even though the Auth password did change
   ✔ 4e an account whose verification failed stays restricted
   ✔ 4f a verified password change lifts the lock and stamps the change
   ✔ 4g and only then is the change recorded, once
   ✔ 4h a proof is single-use: replaying it is refused
   ✔ 4i and the replay adds no second audit row
   ✔ 4j the unlocked member reads their own record and only their own
   ✔ 5a a locked account cannot reset another account, grant itself a role, or grant itself a permission
   ✔ 5b and it is still locked, and still reads nothing
   ✔ 5c an ordinary member, fully signed in, cannot start a reset on another account
   ✔ 6a a reset starts by confining the account, and says only that it was started
   ✔ 6b completion is refused while the Auth password is unchanged, so no reset is ever falsely recorded
   ✔ 6c an aborted reset restores the standing the account had, and records the failure
   ✔ 6d aborting a reset twice raises nothing and changes nothing
   ✔ 6e a completed reset leaves the account locked and records the issue exactly once
   ✔ 6f the reservation is consumed: the same token cannot be completed again
   ✔ 6g no plaintext or temporary password is anywhere in the audit trail
   ✔ 6h in the public schema the only password columns are a flag and a timestamp, never a credential
   ✔ 6i an officer cannot reset their own password here, nor an account that does not exist
   ✔ 7a a change begun before a staff reset cannot complete afterwards
   ✔ 7b so the newer reset stands: the account is still locked
   ✔ 7c a superseded reset cannot be completed by the officer who started it
   ✔ 7d and the account is locked the whole way through
   ✔ 7e an abort carrying a superseded token cannot unlock the account
   ✔ 8a a suspended member who changes their password is still suspended
   ✔ 8b and the session the server reads still reports the suspension, so sign-in is refused
   ✔ 8c a deactivated account that changes its password still holds no permission and reads nothing
   ✔ 9a the three gate functions still refuse an account on a temporary password
   ✔ 9b the retired helpers are stubs that raise, not silent no-ops, and nobody may call them
   ✔ 9c the three reset steps are callable by a signed-in officer, so the database keeps deciding who may reset whom

